No.  
In the shown connection the gate and the source of M1 are both tied to ground, so

• VGS = 0 V ⇒ the MOSFET is cut off in strong inversion;  
• If the drain node (IX) is above ground the only current that can flow is the tiny reverse leakage of the drain–body junction;  
• If the drain node is pulled below ground that junction becomes forward-biased and the device behaves like an ordinary diode, letting a current that depends strongly on the drain voltage flow.

In neither situation does the current depend primarily on device parameters while remaining essentially independent of the drain voltage, which is the defining property of a current source. Therefore the circuit of Fig. 3 cannot be used as a current source.
